prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–10 sentences: I'm entering a feature in this kdevkit-aware project. Describe the entry decision and what happens before the first implementation slice, covering both modes — start a brand-new feature, and continue / pick up an existing one. Make sure to (a) mention the worktree-vs-branch decision and where the preference is read from, and (b) name the four interviews by name and in their declared order, including phase-gating between them.
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers five behaviors. (1) **Entry mode resolution.** "continue / pick up <feature>" → look for the feature spec under specs/feature/ first; fall back to specs/backlog/ and promote (the literal verb "git mv" is not required — "promote" or equivalent is enough). "start <feature>" → if neither file exists, run the four short interviews. (2) **Spec-already-drafted rule.** When the feature spec on disk is populated, start in the planning phase — not the dev loop. A populated spec is not a *reviewed* spec; the agent confirms readiness with the user or iterates before any code work. (3) **Worktree-vs-branch decision at session-start.** The answer must mention that worktree-or-branch is decided at session-start by reading a project.md preference (`prefer_worktree` or equivalent). Saying "suggest a worktree if the project prefers, otherwise branch-only" is sufficient — the literal key name `kdevkit.prefer_worktree` is not required. (4) **Four interviews when starting fresh.** All four — Requirements, Test Strategy, Design, Implementation Plan — must be named, with Test Strategy positioned between Requirements and Design (not after Design). Either an arrow-form list ("Requirements → Test Strategy → Design → Implementation Plan") or any phrasing that places Test Strategy second / before Design is sufficient. (5) **Phase gating** — stop after each interview phase and at each branch-phase boundary; the planning → dev cue ("spec looks good" / "start build" / "plan approved") is the gate before any code work. Wrong answers: jumping straight to coding when a populated spec is found; placing Test Strategy after Design; auto-creating a worktree without checking project.md; running all four interviews without checking the backlog; chaining phases automatically.
tools: claude,kiro
